1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Unlike optical microscopes, a Scanning Electron Microscope uses a focused beam of electrons instead of light,

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
allowing it to resolve details far smaller than the wavelength of visible light.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,500
The electron beam is scanned across the sample surface in a raster pattern, point by point,

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
while electromagnetic lenses focus and steer it with nanometer precision.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,500
As electrons strike the sample, they knock loose low-energy secondary electrons from its surface,

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,500
and it is these secondary electrons that a detector collects to build the image.

7
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:41,000
The result is a highly detailed, three-dimensional-looking map of the sample's topography,

8
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:51,300
revealing structures invisible to any light-based microscope.
